\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\lang9 I know this. I built a Newtonian reference frame model that coincides with Einstein's model where Einstein's model is valid; and outperforms Einstein's model where the assumptions in Einstein's model doesn't reflect what we see from all of our space and ground-based telescopes and radio telescopes. My model uses all of that data and data from CERN and LIGO and has a slot for when LISA comes online. \par
\par
These observational anchors are the testbed for verifying my models accuracy. All of my over 500 simulation models are hitting 100% accuracy across the board. AI was not smart enough to build the models by itself. I had to hold its hand on every derivation and every simulation build; I based my models on proven and uncontested physics in a coordinate system that is constant and where the rate of change of time is 1, meaning that dt/dt=1. These are the fundamental principles of all other physics except spacetime. \par
\par
I solved all of the paradoxes of Einstein's theories by eliminating singularities which I proved cannot exist in nature due to the force balance equation of collapse zones. I started by modeling neutron star collapses and determined the maximum mass density possible based on the physics and properties of neutron stars. The maximum density is right at the highest range for neutron star density. I calculated the gravitational forces and the pressure under these circumstances and wrote a balance equation for the point when collapse can no longer continue because the outward force of pressure exceeds the inward force of gravity. That gave me the Trinca Maximum Mass Density constant of 1.02x10^18 kg/m^3. \par
\par
Then I calculated the radius of the neutron star at that point and the mass. This gave me the Halt Radius (a term I coined) where collapse can no longer increase density. Any additional mass accretion increases the remnant stars radius and mass, which also pushes it over the Trinca Boundary. The Trinca Boudary is a function that describes thje boundary between a neutron star and a black hole with an event horizon. \par
\par
I calculated this by setting the Scwartchild radius equal to the neutron star radius for a non-rotating neutron star, or black hole, that gave me the no-spin case and a mass of 2.88 solar mass. This would be the point where a non-rotating black hole would begin to form an invisibility cloak and where the cloak and the remnant have the same radius. Any additional mass increases the cloak radius faster than the remnant radius, this was based on the mathemntical formulas. I used the term "cloak" to distinguish between Einstein's black hole with a singularity and my model's equivalent with no singularity. In practice the cloak radius and the event horizon radius are equal.  \par
\par
I then used the Kerr black hole solution which accounts for spin or rotatition of the star or black hole remnant. I mapped the boundary vs spin paramater and determined the maximal spin parmater of 1 produced a 1.44 solar mass threshold point on the graph. I calculated multiple points and determinded the function that described the curve, and plotted it as the Trinca Boundary, a threshold where for any given spin paramter there is a corrsponding mass that is still visible on the lower side of the curve and not visible on the upper side of the curve.\par

